<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="ketogenesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>Ketone bodies are carbonyl compounds produced as water-soluble byproducts when fatty acids are broken down for energy in the liver (ketogenesis). There are only three endogenous ketone bodies: |FRAME: 3-KETOBUTYRATE|, |FRAME: CPD-335|, and |FRAME: ACETONE|. Ketogenesis occurs primarily in the mitochondrial matrix of liver cells and is important for providing metabolic fuel for peripheral tissues, especially cardiac muscle and skeletal muscle under exertion. The brain normally uses only glucose as its energy source, but during starvation or glucose dysregulation ketone bodies serve as its primary fuel as fatty-acids can not pass the blood-brain barrier. Ketogenesis is the last step of lipid energy metabolism, a superpathway linking dietary lipids and adipose triglycerides to the TCA cycle and the respiratory chain. This pathway has three highly regulated control points, adipocyte lipolysis, mitochondrial fatty acid entry, and HMGCoA synthase |CITS: [14769483]| Acetoacetate is formed in three reactions: condensation of two molecules of acetyl-CoA by acetyl-CoA tranferase working in the reverse direction from its function as the final step in beta oxidation |CITS: [17371050]|; condensation of acetoacetyl-CoA with a third acetyl-CoA by HMG-CoA synthase |CITS: [10031425]|; and degradation of HMG-CoA to acetoacetate by HMG-CoA lyase |CITS: [8027038]|. Acetoacetate, a &amp;beta;-keto acid, undergoes spontaneous decarboxylation to acetone. The liver releases acetoacetate and D-3-hydroxybutyrate from mitochondria, via |FRAME: ENSG00000155380-MONOMER monocarboxylate transporter 1|, into the bloodstream for distribution to peripheral tissues and brain where ketone body utilization occurs |CITS: [18668440]|. In a potentially life-threatening complication of diabetes mellitus (ketoacidosis) accumulation of these acids can lower blood pH beyond the normal buffering capacity and reduces the ability of hemoglobin to bind oxygen |CITS: [20491249]|. Also when acetoacetate is produced faster than it can be metabolized, a buildup of acetone results, giving the breath of diabetics with poorly controlled glucose levels a characteristic sweet smell |CITS: [9727714]|. Under normal conditions the acetyl-CoA starting material for ketogenesis is generated by beta-oxidation of fatty acids derived from triacylglycerols (TAG) which are metabolized in adipose tissue under the control of |FRAME: HS01328-MONOMER hormone-sensitive lipase| |CITS: [12364542]|. This is balanced with |FRAME: GLUCONEO-PWY gluconeogenesis I|, by directing |FRAME: OXALACETIC_ACID oxaloacetate| out of mitochondria to the cytosol so that acetyl-CoA flux is shifted away from the |FRAME: PWY-5690 TCA cycle variation III (eukaryotic)|, which requires oxaloacetate, and towards ketogenesis |CITS: [1196305]|. During starvation when TAG stores are reduced, certain amino acids can enter the ketogenesis pathway at different points; |FRAME: ILE L-isoleucine| (at acetyl-CoA), |FRAME: PHE L-phenylalanine| and |FRAME: TYR L-tyrosine| (at acetoacetyl-CoA), |FRAME: LEU L-leucine| (at HMG-CoA) and |FRAME: LYS L-lysine| and |FRAME: TRP L-tryptophan| (at acetoacetate).</Description>
